<GuideLayout
  title="How sleeping bag temperature ratings work"
  seoTitle="Sleeping Bag Ratings Explained: EN/ISO Comfort vs Limit"
  description="Sleeping bag ratings explained: what comfort, limit and extreme mean under ISO 23537 and EN 13537, why real-world reviews matter more, and down vs synthetic."
  eyebrow="Sleep system"
  readTime="10 min read"
>
  <p>
    A sleeping bag labelled "0°C" sounds simple. Buy it, take it somewhere that
    drops to 0°C, sleep warm. Plenty of hikers have learned the hard way that it
    rarely works like that. The good news: mild-weather trips are forgiving, and
    a few simple checks get you most of the way.
  </p>
  <p>
    The short answer: treat any temperature rating as a rough starting point, not
    a promise. Everyone sleeps differently, and manufacturer figures, tested or
    not, tend to overestimate how warm a bag feels on a real night in a tent. The
    most useful evidence is real-world reviews from owners who say what the
    overnight temperature actually was and how they felt. Watch out for the number in the product name, too. It is often the
    lower limit, not the comfort figure.
  </p>

  <div class="takeaways">
    <strong>Key takeaways</strong>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Comfort, limit and extreme ratings are a rough starting point. Maker
        figures, tested or not, tend to overestimate real-world warmth.
      </li>
      <li>
        Lean on reviews that mention the actual overnight temperature and how
        warm the owner slept.
      </li>
      <li>
        The temperature in a bag's name is often the lower limit. Look for the
        comfort figure in the specs.
      </li>
      <li>
        For a first overnighter, pick a mild night (around 10°C or warmer) and
        carry a warm layer, whatever the label says.
      </li>
      <li>
        Your <a href="/guides/sleeping-mat-r-value/">sleeping mat's R-value</a> matters
        as much as the bag.
      </li>
      <li>
        Dry sleep clothes, a puffy and a hot water bottle can make any bag
        feel warmer for free.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <h2>What temperature sleeping bag do I need?</h2>
  <p>
    Check the forecast low for your campsite a few days out. For planning further
    ahead, the <a
      href="http://www.bom.gov.au/climate/data/"
      rel="noopener">Bureau of Meteorology climate statistics</a
    > show typical overnight minimums for the nearest station. Then:
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>Take the coldest likely overnight low, not the average.</li>
    <li>
      Subtract a few degrees for elevation, wind or a cold valley campsite.
    </li>
    <li>
      Read reviews of the bag where owners mention the real overnight
      temperatures they slept in and whether they were warm. Treat the label as
      the best case, not the typical night.
    </li>
    <li>
      If you sleep cold, plan to wear warm layers or use a liner.
    </li>
    <li>
      Sleep in the bag in the backyard or on a cool night at home before the trip,
      so you learn whether you run warm or cold.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <p>As a rough guide:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Mild summer or coastal nights:</strong> a bag owners describe as warm
      at around 10°C is plenty. If you get hot, unzip it and use it like a quilt, or
      sleep on top of it.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Spring and autumn:</strong> coastal trips in much of Australia can drop
      to single digits overnight, so look for owners who slept warm on nights
      like that and bring extra layers.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Winter outside alpine areas:</strong> usually mild by international
      standards, especially on the coast. Add a beanie, thermals and extra ground
      insulation.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Alpine areas:</strong> these can fall below freezing in any month,
      and are outside the scope of a first overnighter.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Overheating is a real issue too. A bag that is too warm for the night is
    bulkier, heavier and pricier than you need.
  </p>

  <h2>What do sleeping bag temperature ratings mean?</h2>
  <p>
    Tested bags are measured in a lab and given up to three temperatures:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Comfort:</strong> the lowest temperature at which a standard woman can
      sleep comfortably in a relaxed position. It is the most useful of the three,
      but real people vary a lot around it.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Lower limit (sometimes called "limit" or "transition"):</strong>{' '}
      the lowest temperature at which a standard man can sleep for eight hours curled
      up without waking from cold.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Extreme:</strong> a survival figure. Ignore it.
    </li>
  </ul>

