  <h2>ISO 23537 vs EN 13537: which sleeping bag rating system applies?</h2>
  <p>
    EN 13537 ratings on older bags and <a
      href="https://www.iso.org/standard/67105.html"
      rel="noopener">ISO 23537-1</a
    > ratings on newer ones are broadly comparable. A tested rating lets you compare
    bags on the same basis, but it is measured on a heated mannequin in a lab, not
    by a real person in a tent on a windy night. Many budget bags sold online quote
    a single temperature with no standard named, and those numbers are often even
    more optimistic.
  </p>
  <p>
    Either way, a rating is not a buying rule. How a bag performed for real owners
    on real nights tells you more.
  </p>

  <h2>What is a sleeping bag comfort rating?</h2>
  <p>
    The comfort rating is meant to describe a night you would actually enjoy. The
    lower limit describes a night you get through. In practice many people feel
    cold well above the comfort figure, because everyone sleeps differently. On a <a
      href="/guides/overnight-hike-packing-list/">first overnight hike</a
    >, when you are still working out how warm you sleep, a comfortable night is
    the goal, so plan warmer than the label.
  </p>
  <blockquote>
    Treat the label as a starting point, and trust real owners' nights more.
  </blockquote>
  <p>
    Several things push your real-world result colder than the label: sleeping
    on a thin or <a href="/guides/sleeping-mat-r-value/">uninsulated mat</a>,
    wind through the tent, damp air, being tired or underfed, and simply being a
    cold sleeper.
  </p>

  <h2>Sleeping warmer without buying a warmer bag</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Change into dry sleep clothes and socks before bed.</li>
    <li>Wear your puffy or fleece inside the bag, plus a beanie.</li>
    <li>Eat a snack and go to the toilet before you settle in.</li>
    <li>
      Fill a leak-checked water bottle with hot (not boiling) water and tuck it
      into the bag.
    </li>
    <li>Pull the hood closed around your face.</li>
    <li>
      On your first night, keep the car or the house as a fallback if you get
      cold.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Down vs synthetic sleeping bags: which is better for hiking?</h2>
  <p>
    Down is lighter and packs smaller, but it costs more and needs keeping dry.
    Synthetic is cheaper, tougher and more forgiving. For a first bag, synthetic
    is a fine choice.
  </p>
  <p>
    Both of our kits use the{' '}
    <a href="/gear/400g-down-envelope-sleeping-bag/">400g down envelope sleeping bag</a>{' '}
    because it is light and cheap for down. It has no tested temperature rating
    and seller claims vary, but Australian winters are mild by international
    standards, so outside alpine areas it is perfect for most Australian
    conditions. On colder nights, pair it with an insulated mat and sleep in a
    warm layer.
  </p>
