  <h2>How to choose a sleeping bag for hiking</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Warmth:</strong> use any rating as a rough guide only. Check reviews
      that mention real overnight temperatures close to your expected low.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Shape:</strong> mummy bags are warmer for their weight; rectangular
      bags give more room to move.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Weight and packed size:</strong> check both against your pack. A bag
      over about 1.5 kg starts to dominate a beginner kit.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Length:</strong> your feet should not press against the end, which compresses
      insulation.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Mat pairing:</strong> any bag needs insulation underneath, so budget
      for a suitable mat at the same time.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Our <a href="/#kits">beginner hiking kits</a> pair a bag and mat that are chosen
    to work together, and <a href="/build-my-kit/">Build My Kit</a> matches a bag
    to gear you already own.
  </p>

  <h2>The bottom line</h2>
  <p>
    Know what comfort, limit and extreme mean, but treat any rating as a rough
    starting point. Lean on real owners' experience of actual overnight
    temperatures, and pick a settled forecast for your first trip. Pair the bag with a mat that insulates properly, try it at home first,
    and use dry clothes and a hot water bottle to squeeze out extra warmth.
  </p>

  <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
  <div class="faq">
    <details>
      <summary>Is the comfort or limit rating more important?</summary>
      <p>
        The comfort rating is the more useful of the two, but neither is a
        guarantee. The lower limit describes getting through the night curled up,
        so don't plan around it. Owner reviews matter more than either figure.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>What does a 0°C sleeping bag rating mean?</summary>
      <p>
        It depends which rating the 0°C refers to. The number in a product name is
        often the lower limit, so check the specs for the comfort figure. Even a
        tested 0°C comfort rating is optimistic for many people, and a limit or
        untested figure more so. Expect to feel cold well above 0°C unless owner
        reviews say otherwise.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary
        >Why am I cold in a sleeping bag rated for the temperature?</summary
      >
      <p>
        Ratings tend to overestimate real-world warmth, and everyone sleeps
        differently. Other common causes are an under-insulated mat, using the limit rather than
        comfort rating, damp clothing, wind, or being a cold sleeper. Check the
        mat first; ground contact steals a lot of heat.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>Is down or synthetic better for Australian hiking?</summary>
      <p>
        Down is better when weight and pack space matter and you can keep the
        bag dry. Synthetic is better on a budget or in persistently wet or humid
        conditions, and is a fine choice for a first bag.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>Does a sleeping bag liner add warmth?</summary>
      <p>
        Yes, a liner adds a few degrees of warmth and keeps the bag cleaner.
        Manufacturers' warmth claims for liners vary, so treat them as
        approximate.
      </p>
    </details>
  </div>
